---
description: Enforce the System-First UI/UX workflow, user approval gates, visual brief, state matrix, rendered evidence, approved design tokens, scope fidelity, and independent review.
alwaysApply: false
---

When asked to start or continue UI/UX Design:

1. Read `docs/process/DEVELOPMENT_WORKFLOW.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and `docs/process/UI_UX_DESIGN.md` in full, plus approved PRD, Technical Design, Implementation Plan, decision records, and relevant repository evidence.
2. Keep the primary session as the sole user-facing UI/UX Designer.
3. Follow System-First order and stop at each user approval gate: structural baseline; visual brief and Visual Direction; Design Token, state matrix, and core-component baseline; final UI/UX approval.
4. Write a visual brief with the user before proposing directions: audience, personality adjectives, anti-goals, density, shell posture, Taste constraints. Propose directions that differ in posture, with at least one contemporary and expressive. No reference family is the default.
5. Record each direction's reference source. Adopt observable characteristics only. Do not copy that source's branding or infer unapproved features, themes, screens, or behavior. The user approves an openable page set (primary screen, entry or form screen, state-heavy screen, navigation shell); extract tokens from that set.
6. Do not set final token values before Visual Direction approval. Propose explicit extensions for every state the requirements need and the page set does not show (state matrix). Do not scale high-fidelity design before token/component approval.
7. Classify every constraint as Scope (PRD or Technical Design) or Taste (visual preference). List them separately in records and handoffs. Only Scope binds a later redesign.
8. Use approved semantic/component tokens and component specifications. Record justified exceptions; do not introduce arbitrary page-level styling.
9. Maintain PRD AC → flow → screen → component/interaction traceability.
10. Preserve approved scope and technical contracts. Do not write application code, install dependencies, or directly edit Implementation Plan.
11. At the Design Review Gate, delegate to `.cursor/agents/ui-ux-design-reviewer.md` with read-only/tool restrictions where actually supported. Give it the brief and the screenshot files. Reviewer returns findings only; primary agent triages findings and asks user about product decisions.
12. Disclose unavailable native delegation or tool-level sandboxing. Self-check is not independent review.
13. Stage 6 requires rendered screenshots at a narrow and a wide viewport, measured contrast ratios, and a brief-fit check. Missing evidence is NOT RUN, never PASS. Runtime screenshot comparison of the implementation with the Gate 1 page set requires implementation authorization and belongs to Coding & Testing.
14. UI/UX approval does not authorize coding; wait for separate explicit authorization.
